\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Building a production-grade ArangoDB knowledge graph from 40+ cybersecurity sources\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 A Python-based agentic ingestion engine can reliably seed ~10M nodes and ~28M edges from open cybersecurity and compliance data, then keep them current through scheduled refresh cycles ranging from hourly to quarterly.
\f1\b0  The architecture centers on deterministic 
\f2\fs26 _key
\f1\fs24 -based upserts via 
\f2\fs26 python-arango
\f1\fs24 's 
\f2\fs26 import_bulk()
\f1\fs24  method (100\'962,100\'d7 faster than AQL UPSERT), a plugin-based agent pattern for each data source, and 
\f2\fs26 graphlib.TopologicalSorter
\f1\fs24  for dependency-aware DAG execution. Every data source researched here is freely accessible with minimal or no authentication, and all support some form of incremental update detection \'97 from NVD's 
\f2\fs26 lastModStartDate
\f1\fs24  parameter to OSV's 
\f2\fs26 modified_id.csv
\f1\fs24  changelog to git-based diff detection for ATT&CK, Nuclei, and ExploitDB.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. Vulnerability intelligence APIs: current specifications\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 NVD API 2.0\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The NVD REST API at 
\f2\fs26 https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0
\f1\fs24  serves 
\f0\b 318,000+ CVE records
\f1\b0  with offset pagination (
\f2\fs26 startIndex
\f1\fs24  + 
\f2\fs26 resultsPerPage
\f1\fs24 , max 
\f0\b 2,000 per page
\f1\b0 ). Without an API key, the rate limit is 
\f0\b 5 requests per 30-second rolling window
\f1\b0 ; with a free API key (requested at 
\f2\fs26 nvd.nist.gov/developers/request-an-api-key
\f1\fs24 ), this increases to 
\f0\b 50 requests per 30 seconds
\f1\b0 . The key is passed as an 
\f2\fs26 apiKey
\f1\fs24  HTTP header. NVD recommends sleeping 6 seconds between requests without a key, 2 seconds with one.\
For incremental updates, the 
\f2\fs26 lastModStartDate
\f1\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 lastModEndDate
\f1\fs24  parameters (ISO-8601 UTC) return only CVEs modified within that window. The 
\f0\b maximum date range is 120 days
\f1\b0  \'97 longer gaps must be chunked. NVD recommends polling no more than once every 2 hours. The Change History API at 
\f2\fs26 /cvehistory/2.0
\f1\fs24  provides audit-level detail on what changed. Note that NVD experienced enrichment pauses in 2024 and continues to have intermittent reliability issues (503 errors), making robust retry logic essential.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 OSV.dev, CISA KEV, and EPSS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 OSV.dev
\f1\b0  (
\f2\fs26 api.osv.dev
\f1\fs24 ) requires no authentication and currently imposes 
\f0\b no rate limits
\f1\b0 . The batch endpoint accepts up to 
\f0\b 1,000 queries per request
\f1\b0  and supports ~30 ecosystems (PyPI, npm, Go, Maven, crates.io, Debian, Alpine, Ubuntu, and more). For bulk access, a GCS bucket at 
\f2\fs26 gs://osv-vulnerabilities
\f1\fs24  provides per-ecosystem ZIP downloads and \'97 critically for incremental updates \'97 a 
\f2\fs26 modified_id.csv
\f1\fs24  file sorted in reverse chronological order. Stream this file and stop when reaching your last-processed timestamp.\

\f0\b CISA KEV
\f1\b0  is a static JSON file at 
\f2\fs26 https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json
\f1\fs24  containing 
\f0\b ~1,507 entries
\f1\b0  as of early 2026. It requires no authentication and updates on weekdays during US Eastern business hours. The GitHub mirror at 
\f2\fs26 cisagov/kev-data
\f1\fs24  tracks changes via commit history, enabling git-based delta detection. Each entry includes 
\f2\fs26 cveID
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 vendorProject
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 product
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 dateAdded
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 dueDate
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 knownRansomwareCampaignUse
\f1\fs24 .\

\f0\b EPSS
\f1\b0  from FIRST.org provides daily bulk CSVs at 
\f2\fs26 https://epss.empiricalsecurity.com/epss_scores-YYYY-MM-DD.csv.gz
\f1\fs24  containing all scored CVEs with three fields: 
\f2\fs26 cve
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 epss
\f1\fs24  (probability 0\'961), 
\f2\fs26 percentile
\f1\fs24 . The REST API at 
\f2\fs26 https://api.first.org/data/v1/epss
\f1\fs24  supports batch lookups (comma-separated CVEs, max 2,000 chars), historical queries back to April 2021, and time-series scopes. Rate limit is 
\f0\b 1,000 requests per minute
\f1\b0 , no authentication required.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 VulnCheck and GitHub Advisory Database\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 VulnCheck v3
\f1\b0  at 
\f2\fs26 https://api.vulncheck.com/v3
\f1\fs24  uses Bearer token authentication with a 
\f0\b free Community tier
\f1\b0  providing access to VulnCheck KEV, NVD++ enriched data, and an exploit database (XDB). Rate limit is 
\f0\b 1,000 requests per minute
\f1\b0 . The 
\f2\fs26 /v3/backup/\{index\}
\f1\fs24  endpoint enables complete bulk downloads of any index. Cursor-based pagination supports up to 2,000 results per page.\

\f0\b GitHub Advisory Database
\f1\b0  can be accessed via REST (
\f2\fs26 GET /advisories
\f1\fs24 , max 100 per page) or GraphQL (
\f2\fs26 securityAdvisories
\f1\fs24  query). With a PAT, rate limits are 
\f0\b 5,000 requests per hour
\f1\b0  (REST) or 
\f0\b 5,000 points per hour
\f1\b0  (GraphQL). For bulk mirroring, cloning the 
\f2\fs26 github/advisory-database
\f1\fs24  repo (OSV format) is most efficient, with git pull for incremental updates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Exploit intelligence and MITRE framework data structures\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Metasploit, ExploitDB, Nuclei, and PoC-in-GitHub\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Metasploit
\f1\b0 's 
\f2\fs26 modules_metadata_base.json
\f1\fs24  (~40\'9660 MB) lives at 
\f2\fs26 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rapid7/metasploit-framework/master/db/modules_metadata_base.json
\f1\fs24 . Each module has a 
\f2\fs26 references
\f1\fs24  array where CVEs appear as 
\f2\fs26 "CVE-2024-XXXXX"
\f1\fs24  strings alongside EDB, ZDI, and URL references. Extract via 
\f2\fs26 [ref for ref in module['references'] if ref.startswith('CVE-')]
\f1\fs24 .\

\f0\b ExploitDB
\f1\b0 's 
\f2\fs26 files_exploits.csv
\f1\fs24  at 
\f2\fs26 https://gitlab.com/exploit-database/exploitdb/-/raw/main/files_exploits.csv
\f1\fs24  has 17 columns including a 
\f2\fs26 codes
\f1\fs24  column with semicolon-separated references (e.g., 
\f2\fs26 CVE-2021-44228;OSVDB-12345
\f1\fs24 ). The database contains 
\f0\b 45,000\'9650,000+ exploits
\f1\b0  with daily git updates at 05:05 UTC.\

\f0\b Nuclei Templates
\f1\b0  (
\f2\fs26 projectdiscovery/nuclei-templates
\f1\fs24 ) organize CVE templates under 
\f2\fs26 http/cves/YYYY/CVE-YYYY-NNNNN.yaml
\f1\fs24 . The YAML 
\f2\fs26 classification
\f1\fs24  block provides 
\f2\fs26 cve-id
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 cwe-id
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 cvss-score
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 cvss-metrics
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 epss-score
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 epss-percentile
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 cpe
\f1\fs24  \'97 making each template a rich metadata source. Over 
\f0\b 9,000 total templates
\f1\b0  exist, with 1,496 covering KEV entries. Use 
\f2\fs26 yaml.CSafeLoader
\f1\fs24  for high-performance bulk parsing.\

\f0\b PoC-in-GitHub
\f1\b0  (
\f2\fs26 nomi-sec/PoC-in-GitHub
\f1\fs24 ) stores one JSON file per CVE organized by year (
\f2\fs26 2025/CVE-2025-31324.json
\f1\fs24 ), each containing an array of GitHub repository metadata with 
\f2\fs26 stargazers_count
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 forks_count
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 pushed_at
\f1\fs24  timestamps.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 ATT&CK, CAPEC, D3FEND, ATLAS, and CTID\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 ATT&CK STIX 2.1
\f1\b0  data (current version 
\f0\b v18.1
\f1\b0 ) lives at 
\f2\fs26 mitre-attack/attack-stix-data
\f1\fs24  with JSON bundles per domain (
\f2\fs26 enterprise-attack.json
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 mobile-attack.json
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 ics-attack.json
\f1\fs24 ). Enterprise ATT&CK v18 includes 
\f0\b 216 techniques, 475 sub-techniques, 176 groups, 784 software entries
\f1\b0 , and newly added detection strategies and analytics. STIX object types include 
\f2\fs26 attack-pattern
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 malware
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 tool
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 intrusion-set
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 campaign
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 course-of-action
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 relationship
\f1\fs24 . Version detection uses the 
\f2\fs26 x_mitre_version
\f1\fs24  field in the 
\f2\fs26 x-mitre-collection
\f1\fs24  object.\

\f0\b CAPEC XML
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 3.9
\f1\b0 , 
\f0\b 559 attack patterns
\f1\b0 ) at 
\f2\fs26 https://capec.mitre.org/data/xml/capec_latest.xml
\f1\fs24  maps to ATT&CK via 
\f2\fs26 <Taxonomy_Mapping Taxonomy_Name="ATTACK">
\f1\fs24  elements and to CWE via 
\f2\fs26 <Related_Weakness CWE_ID="89"/>
\f1\fs24  elements.\

\f0\b D3FEND
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 1.3.0
\f1\b0 , 
\f0\b 267 defensive techniques
\f1\b0 ) offers a REST API at 
\f2\fs26 https://d3fend.mitre.org/api/
\f1\fs24  with endpoints like 
\f2\fs26 /api/offensive-technique/attack/\{T_ID\}.json
\f1\fs24  for ATT&CK-to-D3FEND mappings and 
\f2\fs26 /api/weakness/cwe/\{CWE-ID\}.json
\f1\fs24  for CWE associations. No authentication required.\

\f0\b ATLAS
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 4.4.0
\f1\b0 ) at 
\f2\fs26 mitre-atlas/atlas-data
\f1\fs24  provides 
\f2\fs26 dist/ATLAS.yaml
\f1\fs24  with 12 tactics, 40 techniques, and 27 sub-techniques for AI systems. ATT&CK cross-references appear as 
\f2\fs26 ATT&CK-reference
\f1\fs24  keys with technique IDs and URLs.\

\f0\b CTID Mappings
\f1\b0  (now at 
\f2\fs26 center-for-threat-informed-defense/mappings-explorer
\f1\fs24 ) provide ATT&CK 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  NIST 800-53 Rev 5 relationships as STIX bundles. Controls are 
\f2\fs26 course-of-action
\f1\fs24  objects; mappings are 
\f2\fs26 relationship
\f1\fs24  objects with 
\f2\fs26 relationship_type: "mitigates"
\f1\fs24  linking controls to 
\f2\fs26 attack-pattern
\f1\fs24  targets. Current mappings cover ATT&CK v16.1, with v18 updates forthcoming.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Compliance frameworks and package ecosystem endpoints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 NIST OSCAL, SCF, OLIR, OpenCRE, and CWE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 NIST OSCAL SP 800-53 Rev 5
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 5.2.0
\f1\b0 , OSCAL 
\f0\b 1.1.3
\f1\b0 ) is available at 
\f2\fs26 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/usnistgov/oscal-content/main/nist.gov/SP800-53/rev5/json/NIST_SP-800-53_rev5_catalog.json
\f1\fs24 . The JSON hierarchy follows 
\f2\fs26 catalog 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  groups (20 families) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  controls 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  controls (enhancements) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  parts (statement, guidance, objective)
\f1\fs24 . Approximately 
\f0\b 1,007 controls and enhancements
\f1\b0  span families AC through SR. Parameters use 
\f2\fs26 \{\{ insert: param, param-id \}\}
\f1\fs24  notation in prose text.\

\f0\b SCF
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 2025.4
\f1\b0 ) is downloadable without registration from GitHub Releases at 
\f2\fs26 github.com/securecontrolsframework/securecontrolsframework/releases
\f1\fs24 . The Excel file maps controls to 
\f0\b 100+ frameworks
\f1\b0  including ISO 27001:2022, NIST CSF, PCI-DSS, HIPAA, SOC 2, CMMC, GDPR, and FedRAMP. The primary sheet contains SCF ID, Domain, Control Description, and one column per mapped framework.\

\f0\b NIST OLIR
\f1\b0  CSF 2.0 
\f3 \uc0\u8596 
\f1  SP 800-53 mappings are available as Excel at 
\f2\fs26 https://csrc.nist.gov/files/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final/docs/csf-pf-to-sp800-53r5-mappings.xlsx
\f1\fs24 . Additional mappings (ISO/IEC 27001:2022, CSF 1.1, Privacy Framework) are available from the same NIST page.\

\f0\b OpenCRE
\f1\b0  at 
\f2\fs26 https://www.opencre.org/rest/v1/
\f1\fs24  provides endpoints for 
\f2\fs26 root_cres
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 cre/\{id\}
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 standard/\{name\}
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 text_search
\f1\fs24 . CREs act as intermediary nodes linking OWASP ASVS, NIST 800-53, CWE, ISO 27001, and other standards through a hierarchical concept graph. No authentication required.\

\f0\b CWE
\f1\b0  (version 
\f0\b 4.19.1
\f1\b0 ) downloads as 
\f2\fs26 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/xml/cwec_latest.xml.zip
\f1\fs24  with 
\f0\b ~930+ individual weakness entries
\f1\b0 . View 1424 provides IEC 62443 mappings. Updates are quarterly. The XML root element's 
\f2\fs26 Version
\f1\fs24  attribute enables version comparison for change detection.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Package and distribution security data\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 deps.dev
\f1\b0  (Google) at 
\f2\fs26 https://api.deps.dev/v3/
\f1\fs24  covers 
\f0\b 7 ecosystems
\f1\b0  (Go, npm, Cargo, Maven, PyPI, NuGet, RubyGems) with no authentication. The v3alpha API adds PURL support via 
\f2\fs26 PurlLookup
\f1\fs24 /
\f2\fs26 PurlLookupBatch
\f1\fs24 . Data includes licenses (SPDX), advisories (OSV IDs), SLSA provenance, and OpenSSF Scorecards.\

\f0\b Ecosyste.ms
\f1\b0  operates 
\f0\b 16+ microservices
\f1\b0  with the most relevant being Packages (75 registries), Advisories (24K+ advisories), Repositories, and License Parser. Rate limit is 
\f0\b 5,000 requests per hour
\f1\b0  with a priority "polite pool" for requests with a 
\f2\fs26 From
\f1\fs24  header. PURL lookups are natively supported.\

\f0\b endoflife.date
\f1\b0  tracks 
\f0\b 432+ products
\f1\b0  at 
\f2\fs26 https://endoflife.date/api/v1/products/
\f1\fs24  with no authentication. Each cycle returns 
\f2\fs26 releaseDate
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 eol
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 latest
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 lts
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 support
\f1\fs24  dates.\

\f0\b Red Hat Security Data API
\f1\b0  at 
\f2\fs26 https://access.redhat.com/hydra/rest/securitydata/cve.json
\f1\fs24  supports filtering by severity, package, product, date range, and CWE. 
\f0\b Debian Security Tracker
\f1\b0  provides a ~30 MB JSON dump at 
\f2\fs26 https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/data/json
\f1\fs24  with per-release fix status. 
\f0\b Ubuntu
\f1\b0 's CVE API is still marked work-in-progress; OVAL data files are the more reliable automated option.\

\f0\b SPDX License List
\f1\b0  JSON at 
\f2\fs26 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spdx/license-list-data/main/json/licenses.json
\f1\fs24  includes 
\f2\fs26 licenseId
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 name
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 isOsiApproved
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 isFsfLibre
\f1\fs24  for all SPDX licenses. 
\f0\b OSADL
\f1\b0  provides its compatibility matrix as CSV at 
\f2\fs26 https://www.osadl.org/fileadmin/checklists/unreflicenses/compatibility.csv
\f1\fs24  or via 
\f2\fs26 pip install osadl-matrix
\f1\fs24  for Python-native access.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Python ingestion architecture and ArangoDB optimization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 The driver stack and bulk loading strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 python-arango
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  8.3.0
\f1\b0  (February 2026) is the recommended synchronous driver, maintained by ArangoDB officially. For async operations, 
\f5\b\fs26 \strokec2 python-arango-async
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  0.5.4
\f1\b0  (the official async driver, import name 
\f2\fs26 arangoasync
\f1\fs24 ) replaces the deprecated community 
\f2\fs26 aioarango
\f1\fs24 . Use synchronous 
\f2\fs26 python-arango
\f1\fs24  for bulk loading and async for concurrent API fetching during incremental updates.\
The 
\f0\b fastest programmatic bulk loading method
\f1\b0  is 
\f2\fs26 collection.import_bulk()
\f1\fs24 , which wraps ArangoDB's 
\f2\fs26 /_api/import
\f1\fs24  endpoint. It accepts a list of dicts and supports 
\f2\fs26 on_duplicate
\f1\fs24  parameter with values 
\f2\fs26 "update"
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 "replace"
\f1\fs24 , or 
\f2\fs26 "ignore"
\f1\fs24  \'97 providing native upsert behavior when documents use deterministic 
\f2\fs26 _key
\f1\fs24  values. 
\f0\b Critical benchmark finding
\f1\b0 : 
\f2\fs26 import_bulk()
\f1\fs24  completes 1,000 documents in ~0.03 seconds, while AQL 
\f2\fs26 UPSERT
\f1\fs24  takes ~6 seconds for the same \'97 a 
\f0\b 200\'d7 performance gap
\f1\b0  that widens further with sharding.\
Recommended batch sizes are 
\f0\b 50,000\'96100,000 documents
\f1\b0  per call. For the initial 10M-node seed, the optimal strategy is:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Drop all non-primary, non-edge indexes\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bulk load via 
\f2\fs26 import_bulk()
\f1\fs24  in 50K batches (or 
\f2\fs26 arangoimport
\f1\fs24  CLI for JSONL files)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rebuild indexes after load completes\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Enable 
\f2\fs26 on_duplicate="update"
\f1\fs24  for incremental updates without index dropping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Derive deterministic 
\f2\fs26 _key
\f1\fs24  values from natural identifiers: 
\f2\fs26 "CVE-2024-1234"
\f1\fs24  for CVEs, 
\f2\fs26 "CWE-79"
\f1\fs24  for weaknesses, 
\f2\fs26 "T1190"
\f1\fs24  for ATT&CK techniques. For edges, use composite keys like 
\f2\fs26 "CVE-2024-1234_affects_pkg:npm/express@4.18.2"
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Rate limiting, scheduling, retries, and XML parsing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 pyrate-limiter
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  4.0.2
\f1\b0  is the best choice for multi-source rate limiting, supporting multiple rate definitions per resource with sync and async modes. Define per-source limiters matching each API's constraints (e.g., 
\f2\fs26 Rate(50, Duration.SECOND * 30)
\f1\fs24  for NVD with key, 
\f2\fs26 Rate(5000, Duration.HOUR)
\f1\fs24  for GitHub).\
For 
\f0\b scheduling
\f1\b0 , 
\f0\b Prefect 3.x
\f1\b0  provides the best balance of Python-native development, built-in scheduling with cron syntax, automatic retries, and a rich monitoring UI \'97 without requiring external infrastructure like Redis or RabbitMQ. Flows are defined via simple decorators (
\f2\fs26 @flow
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 @task
\f1\fs24 ) and deployed with cron expressions. For simpler deployments, 
\f0\b APScheduler
\f1\b0  with a 
\f2\fs26 BackgroundScheduler
\f1\fs24  and SQLAlchemy job store provides crash recovery without any external dependencies.\

\f5\b\fs26 \strokec2 tenacity
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  9.1.4
\f1\b0  handles retries with 
\f2\fs26 wait_exponential_jitter(initial=1, max=60)
\f1\fs24  for API calls and ArangoDB operations. The 
\f2\fs26 retry_if_exception_type
\f1\fs24  predicate should target 
\f2\fs26 ArangoServerError
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 ArangoConnectionError
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 ConnectionError
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 TimeoutError
\f1\fs24 . Use 
\f2\fs26 before_log
\f1\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 after_log
\f1\fs24  callbacks for observability.\
For 
\f0\b 50 MB+ CWE/CAPEC XML files
\f1\b0 , 
\f2\fs26 lxml.etree.iterparse()
\f1\fs24  is 2\'9610\'d7 faster than stdlib 
\f2\fs26 ElementTree
\f1\fs24  for full parsing and provides superior namespace handling. The critical pattern is filtering by tag (
\f2\fs26 tag='\{namespace\}Weakness'
\f1\fs24 ), calling 
\f2\fs26 elem.clear()
\f1\fs24  after processing each element, and deleting preceding siblings (
\f2\fs26 del elem.getparent()[0]
\f1\fs24 ) to maintain constant memory usage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 ArangoDB indexing and capacity planning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 In ArangoDB 3.10+, 
\f2\fs26 hash
\f1\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 skiplist
\f1\fs24  indexes are 
\f0\b aliases for 
\f5\fs26 \strokec2 persistent
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2  \'97 there is only one user-creatable index type, backed by a B-tree with optional in-memory cache. Enable 
\f2\fs26 cacheEnabled: true
\f1\fs24  on persistent indexes used for equality lookups (e.g., 
\f2\fs26 cve_id
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 purl
\f1\fs24 ). Use 
\f2\fs26 storedValues
\f1\fs24  to cover projections and avoid document lookups. 
\f0\b Vertex-centric indexes
\f1\b0  (composite on edge 
\f2\fs26 _from
\f1\fs24  + timestamp) are critical for supernodes with 10K+ edges.\
For 
\f0\b 10M nodes + 28M edges
\f1\b0 , expect 
\f0\b 15\'9625 GB on disk
\f1\b0  (with LZ4 compression) and recommend 
\f0\b 32 GB RAM
\f1\b0  (4\'968 GB for RocksDB block cache, 1.5 GB for write buffers, 2\'964 GB for index caches). A 
\f0\b single server is sufficient
\f1\b0  \'97 ArangoDB handles 100M+ documents on one node. Cluster only if high availability is required, and note that UPSERT is non-atomic across shards.\
RocksDB tuning for bulk writes should increase 
\f2\fs26 write-buffer-size
\f1\fs24  to 256 MB, 
\f2\fs26 max-write-buffer-number
\f1\fs24  to 6, and 
\f2\fs26 level0-compaction-trigger
\f1\fs24  to 8. Set 
\f2\fs26 max-background-jobs
\f1\fs24  to match CPU core count and allocate at least 4 GB to 
\f2\fs26 block-cache-size
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 5. Orchestration, dependency ordering, and deployment\
